body:
> I need to look at aiosqlite's new implementation (great news!) but we would also really benefit from having a non-async "terminate()" method of some kind so that when we detect that a connection has been thrown away (which we detect in a non-async weakref handler), we can ensure this connection is closed out without requiring the use of await.

@zzzeek I've put up https://github.com/omnilib/aiosqlite/pull/370 to promote the new stop mechanism to a supported API, which supports being called without an async context, but doesn't guarantee the background thread will be completed before `stop()` returns. If an async event loop is still available, `stop()` will return a future that can be awaited for that purpose. 

Would that be sufficient?
